name: exit status and the special variables
boss: AndOr
flavor: Exit zero and you pass. Anything else is mine.
syntax: ||, $?, $$, $!, &, $#, $@, $*, $0, $1..$9, --, ( ), { }, \
brief: Every command returns a number. Zero means success; anything else is a
brief: failure the command chose. `$?` holds the last one.
brief:
brief: That number is what `&&` and `||` branch on. `cmd1 && cmd2` runs the second
brief: only if the first succeeded; `cmd1 || cmd2` only if it failed. This is
brief: the smallest conditional the shell has, and the most used.
brief:
brief: `true` and `false` do nothing but return a status, which is how you test a
brief: chain without doing any work. `exit N` ends a shell with the status you
brief: pick. `set -e` stops a script at the first command that fails, instead of
brief: carrying on with wrong data.
brief:
brief: The other specials: `$$` this shell's pid, `$!` the last background pid,
brief: `$#` how many arguments, `$@` all of them, `$0` the name it was invoked as.
brief:
brief: `$*` joins that same list into one word instead of keeping every piece
brief: separate. `--` marks the end of options, so anything after it is a
brief: literal argument even if it starts with `-`. `( )` run their commands in
brief: a fresh subshell -- a copy that can't change the parent; `{ }` run them
brief: in the current shell, just grouped as one.
---
q: {run false, then %show% the exit status it left behind in $?|run false and check what $? comes back as|execute false, then %show% its exit code from $?}
a: false; echo $?
cmp: ref
ref: false
teach: false
hint: false always exits 1; it has no other job
---
q: {run true, then %show% the exit status it left behind in $?|run true and verify $? comes back as 0|execute true, then %show% its exit code from $?}
a: true; echo $?
cmp: ref
ref: true
teach: true
hint: true always exits 0, no matter what
---
setup: a script needs to know something is there without any output leaking through
q: %show% the word @word@ only if @log@ contains @sev@, using grep
a: grep -q @sev@ @log@ && echo @word@
out: @word@
cmp: ref
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: -q silences grep's output, leaving only its exit status for &&
---
setup: the absence is the thing worth reporting here, not the presence
q: %show% "missing" if @log@ has no match for nonexistent, with grep
a: grep -q nonexistent @log@ || echo missing
out: ^missing$
cmp: ref
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: || runs its right side only when the left side fails
---
setup: the script has to say something either way, and it gets one line to do it
q: %show% found if @needle@ is in @log@, else missing, with grep
a: grep -q @needle@ @log@ && echo found || echo missing
cmp: ref
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: chain && and || together to cover both outcomes in one line
---
setup: a pid-named lockfile keeps two copies of a script from colliding
q: %show% this shell's pid to use in the lockfile name
a: echo $$
out: ^[1-9][0-9]*$
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: $$ is this shell's own pid, fixed for its whole lifetime
---
q: {start sleep 1 in the background and print the pid it was given|background sleep 1, then print its pid|run sleep 1 as a background job and show what pid it got}
a: sleep 1 & echo $!
out: ^[1-9][0-9]*$
ref: sleep
teach: sleep
hint: an ampersand backgrounds; $! holds the pid it was just given
---
q: {exit 0 so a caller checking $? sees success|end the shell with a clean, successful exit status|leave the shell reporting success via exit status 0}
a: exit 0
status: 0
ref: exit
teach: exit
hint: exit takes the status as its argument; with none it reuses $?
---
q: {exit with status 3 so a caller checking $? knows the failure|end the shell with exit status 3, a custom failure code|leave the shell with exit status 3, a signal to the caller}
a: exit 3
status: 3
ref: exit
teach: exit
hint: any number 0-255; zero means success and everything else is yours to define
---
setup: this step should pass only when the thing is NOT there -- a guard, not a search
q: {grep @log@ for nonexistent, succeed only if there's no match|flip grep's status so no match for nonexistent in @log@ succeeds|with grep, make a missing nonexistent match in @log@ succeed}
a: ! grep -q nonexistent @log@
status: 0
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: a leading ! flips the status
---
q: make a broken step stop the script instead of continuing, with set
a: set -e
why: conceptual
ref: set
teach: set -e
hint: -e makes any failing command end the script immediately
---
q: catch a typo'd variable expanding to empty -- fail loudly, with set
a: set -u
why: conceptual
ref: set
teach: set -u
hint: -u turns a reference to an unset variable into a hard error
---
q: {cd into @word@-place, or create it if that fails|enter @word@-place, falling back to creating it if cd fails|cd into @word@-place or, failing that, make it}
a: cd @word@-place || mkdir @word@-place
state: exists:@word@-place
ref: cd
teach: cd
hint: || only runs its right side when the left side's exit status is nonzero -- cd fails cleanly when the target is missing
---
q: make the shell show each command as it runs, for debugging, with set
a: set -x
why: conceptual
ref: set
teach: set -x
hint: this is the shell debugger, and it is usually enough
---
q: {turn command tracing back off|stop tracing each command}, with set
a: set +x
why: conceptual
ref: set
teach: set
hint: + turns an option off, the mirror of - turning it on
---
setup: you don't know which options this shell already has switched on
q: %show% the shell's current option settings, with set
a: set -o
out: errexit
ref: set
teach: set -o
hint: -o with no name lists every option and whether it's on or off
---
q: set -- a b c, then %show% how many positional parameters there are
a: set -- a b c; echo $#
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: $# counts whatever set -- just loaded -- three words in, 3 comes back
---
q: set -- a b c, then %show% just the second parameter
a: set -- a b c; echo $2
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: $1 is the first positional parameter, $2 the second, on through $9
---
q: set -- "a b" c, then %show% each with printf, one per line
a: set -- "a b" c; printf '%s\n' "$@"
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: "$@" keeps each positional parameter as its own word, spaces and all
---
q: set -- "a b" c, then %show% them as one word with printf, using $*
a: set -- "a b" c; printf '%s\n' "$*"
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: "$*" joins every positional parameter into a single word -- $@ keeps them apart
---
q: run sh -c to echo $0, invoked under the name myscript
a: sh -c "echo \$0" myscript
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: extra words after sh -c 'CMD' become $0, $1, $2... -- myscript lands in $0
---
q: set x to 1, then 2 inside a subshell, then %show% x
a: x=1; ( x=2 ); echo $x
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: ( ) forks a subshell -- x=2 in there is a copy; the outer x is still 1
---
q: set x to 1, then 2 inside a { } group, then %show% x
a: x=1; { x=2; }; echo $x
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: { } runs in the current shell -- x=2 here really overwrites the outer x
---
setup: an argument you need to pass looks exactly like a flag
q: load -n as a plain argument with set, then print it back with printf
a: set -- -n; printf '%s\n' "$1"
cmp: ref
ref: printf
teach: printf
hint: -- marks the end of options, so -n after it is read as a plain word
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: set -- "a b" c; echo $#
a: 2
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: the quotes keep a b as one parameter, so there are two, not three
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: x=1; ( x=2 ); echo $x
a: 1
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: the parens run in a subshell, and nothing it sets survives the closing one
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: x=1; { x=2; }; echo $x
a: 2
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: braces group without a subshell, so the assignment is this shell's
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: set -- "a b" c; printf '%s\n' "$*"
a: a b c
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: "$*" joins every parameter into one word, so printf gets a single line
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: set -- "a b" c; printf '%s\n' "$@" | wc -l
a: 2
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: quoted, "$@" keeps "a b" and c as two separate parameters, so printf runs twice
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: set -- "a b" c; for w in $@; do echo $w; done | wc -l
a: 3
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: unquoted, $@ splits on whitespace just like $* does -- "a b" breaks into two words
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: false | true; echo $?
a: 0
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: $? after a pipeline is the LAST command's status -- true wins even though false ran too
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: true | false; echo $?
a: 1
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: same rule, flipped -- the last command in the pipeline is false, so that's the status kept
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: false && echo a || echo b
a: b
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: && short-circuits on the failure, so its echo never runs -- || is what's left to fire
